        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}', 'javascript' %>

### Review options

When a return's `state` is `pending_merchant_review`, the return object includes a
`review_options` list. Each element represents one action the merchant can take on this return —
use it to present the available actions and to know what to submit back to the
[review endpoint](#review-a-return).

Which options appear depends on why the customer requested the return — see the three cases
below. For where `review_options` sits in the full response, see the
[return object](#return-object-attributes-appendix).

Return reason | Category
------------- | --------
`withdrawal` | [Withdrawal-policy return](#withdrawal-policy-return)
`missing_part` | [Missing-part return](#missing-part-return)
`faulty` | [Faulty-product return](#faulty-product-return)

Every other `return_reason` skips merchant review entirely.

#### Faulty-product return

<%= render_code '{
  "review_options": [
    { "option": "approve" },
    { "option": "contact_manufacturer", "comment_required": true },
    { "option": "resolved_via_customer_communication", "comment_required": false },
    { "option": "usability_suggestions", "comment_required": true },
    { "option": "not_faulty", "comment_required": true }
  ]
}', 'javascript' %>

Option | Meaning
------ | -------
`approve` | accepts the return as a genuinely faulty product
`contact_manufacturer` | reject: this is a manufacturer/service-provider warranty issue, not something the merchant handles directly
`resolved_via_customer_communication` | reject: the merchant already sorted it out directly with the customer (no comment required)
`usability_suggestions` | reject: the product was used in a way that affected its own function/operation
`not_faulty` | reject: the product isn't actually defective; it operates within its stated specifications

#### Missing-part return

<%= render_code '{
  "review_options": [
    { "option": "approve" },
    { "option": "send_missing_part" },
    { "option": "resolved_via_customer_communication", "comment_required": false },
    { "option": "part_not_included", "comment_required": true, "support_proposed_part_url": true },
    { "option": "incorrect_skroutz_content", "comment_required": true }
  ]
}', 'javascript' %>

Option | Meaning
------ | -------
`approve` | accepts the return (refund/replacement go through the normal return flow)
`send_missing_part` | the merchant will ship the missing part directly to the customer instead — this cancels the return itself rather than rejecting it; only offered when the line item isn't fulfilled by Skroutz cargo
`resolved_via_customer_communication` | reject: the merchant already sorted it out directly with the customer (no comment required)
`part_not_included` | reject: the product was delivered complete as described, nothing is actually missing; may optionally include `proposed_part_url` pointing the customer at the correct part
`incorrect_skroutz_content` | reject, but only updates the return request review and leaves the return request itself pending — Skroutz's own catalog data was wrong (a missing part was listed in content that isn't actually included), not the merchant's fault, and needs a correction on the product page

#### Withdrawal-policy return

<%= render_code '{
  "review_options": [
    { "option": "approve" },
    { "option": "out_of_policy_withdrawal", "comment_required": false }
  ]
}', 'javascript' %>

Option | Meaning
------ | -------
`approve` | accepts the withdrawal
`out_of_policy_withdrawal` | reject: the withdrawal falls outside what's allowed (e.g. past the return window, or the item is no longer in its original unopened condition); `comment_required` is `false` because a canned comment is filled in automatically

Only ever these two options for a withdrawal return, regardless of which specific
withdrawal/wrong-size scenario triggered the review — wrong-size reviews follow the same
withdrawal policy and render the same way.

#### Option fields

Each entry in `review_options` has the following fields:

Field | Type | Description
----- | ---- | -----------
`option` | String | the action to submit back to the [review endpoint](#review-a-return)
`comment_required` | Boolean | `true` if a non-blank `comment` must be submitted with this option, `false` if optional
`support_proposed_part_url` | Boolean | present, and only ever `true`, on `part_not_included` — the only option a `proposed_part_url` may be submitted alongside. Omitted on every other option

### Review a return

Resolve a return that is `pending_merchant_review` by submitting one of the options from that
return's current [`review_options`](#review-options).

**Prerequisites:**

* Return must be in `pending_merchant_review` state
* `option` must be one of the values currently listed in the return's `review_options`

<pre class="terminal">
  POST /merchants/ecommerce/returns/:return_code/review
</pre>

To resolve a return you need to provide `option`, which must be one of the strings from this
return's current `review_options`. A `comment` is also required when that option's
`comment_required` is `true`. You can also specify `proposed_part_url`, which is only used when
the option's `support_proposed_part_url` is `true`; it's silently dropped otherwise.

Approving, rejecting, and sending the missing part are the same underlying decision — you can
only do one of them once. After a successful call, `state` moves off `pending_merchant_review`
(to `returning` on approve, `rejected` on reject; `send_missing_part` cancels the return itself,
since the customer is getting the missing part directly rather than a replacement/refund through
the return), the next `merchant_return_updated` [webhook](/smart_cart/returns_webhook) reflects
that, and `review_options` disappears from the payload — there is nothing left to resolve.

#### Example: rejecting a faulty return

<pre class="terminal">
curl -X POST https://<%= settings.api_domain %>/merchants/ecommerce/returns/R-111111-1111111/review \
  -H 'Accept: application/vnd.<%= settings.site_name %>+json; version=3.0' \
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json; charset=utf-8' \
  -H 'Authorization: Bearer your_access_token_here' \
  -d '{ "option": "not_faulty", "comment": "The product works within its stated specifications." }'
</pre>

#### Example: approving a withdrawal return

<pre class="terminal">
curl -X POST https://<%= settings.api_domain %>/merchants/ecommerce/returns/R-111111-1111112/review \
  -H 'Accept: application/vnd.<%= settings.site_name %>+json; version=3.0' \
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json; charset=utf-8' \
  -H 'Authorization: Bearer your_access_token_here' \
  -d '{ "option": "approve" }'
</pre>

#### Success Response

<pre class="terminal">
HTTP/1.1 200 OK
Content-Type: application/json
</pre>

<%= render_code '{
  "success": true
}', 'javascript' %>

#### Error Responses

**Invalid or missing option** (not one of this return's current `review_options`, or the return
has since moved on):

<pre class="terminal">
HTTP/1.1 422 Unprocessable Entity
</pre>

<%= render_code '{
  "errors": [
    {
      "code": "option",
      "messages": [
        "Invalid or missing option."
      ]
    }
  ]
}', 'javascript' %>

**Missing required comment** (the option's `comment_required` is `true` and `comment` was blank
or missing):

<pre class="terminal">
HTTP/1.1 422 Unprocessable Entity
</pre>

<%= render_code '{
  "errors": [
    {
      "code": "comment",
      "messages": [
        "Comment is required for this option."
      ]
    }
  ]
}', 'javascript' %>

**Return no longer pending review** (its `state` moved out of `pending_merchant_review` in the
meantime):

<pre class="terminal">
HTTP/1.1 422 Unprocessable Entity
</pre>

<%= render_code '{
  "errors": [
    {
      "code": "return_status",
      "messages": [
        "Return is not pending merchant review."
      ]
    }
  ]
}', 'javascript' %>

If the option itself was valid but the decision still fails a model-level check (e.g. a
`comment` over 1000 characters), `code` names the failing attribute instead (e.g.
`rejection_comment_sanitized`).

## Error handling

In case of errors, the response will have a representative HTTP status code
in the `4xx` or `5xx` range. The body would contain an array of `errors`.

For example, if the requested return code does not exist (or does not belong to your shop),
you will get a `404` HTTP status code (Not Found):

<%= render_code '{
  "errors": [
    {
      "code": "not_found",
      "messages": [
        "Return not found"
      ]
    }
  ]
}', 'javascript' %>

## Test requests (sandbox)

The following three d​emo return codes​ are available on production for testing reasons — one
for each [review options](#review-options) case. They work against any shop's own API token,
with both `GET` and `POST .../review`.

> It is safe to issue requests for the following demo return codes multiple times for testing,
> since they do not reference any real data. `GET` always returns the fixed `review_options`
> shown below for that case. `POST .../review` accepts any option from that same list (and
> validates `comment` the same way a real return would), but always just responds with
> `{ "success": true }` without changing any data — nothing is persisted, so the same demo code
> can be posted to repeatedly.

Return code | Case
----------- | ----
`DEMO-RETURN` | faulty-product
`DEMO-RETURN-MISSING-PART` | missing-part
`DEMO-RETURN-WITHDRAWAL` | withdrawal-policy

